     <item>
      <widget class="QGroupBox" name="groupBox_2">
       <property name="title">
        <string>Active Markers</string>
       </property>
       <layout class="QGridLayout" name="gridLayout_4">
        <item row="0" column="0">
         <widget class="QTableView" name="activeMarkers">
          <property name="maximumSize">
           <size>
            <width>16777215</width>
            <height>100</height>
           </size>
          </property>
          <property name="whatsThis">
           <string>&lt;p&gt;Control which markers are enabled for this furniture form. The available markers are defined in the 3D model file, via the FRN block.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When looking through Skyrim's furniture objects, you may notice that a lot of them have variants based on entry points, e.g. &lt;code&gt;CommonWritingChair01L&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;CommonWritingChair01R&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;CommonWritingChair01LR&lt;/code&gt;. This is because the game doesn't use collision checks to block access to furniture markers: if a furniture is placed flush against an obstacle, actors can still enter and exit the furniture through the obstacle by interacting with it. Bethesda's solution to this problem is to create variants on each furniture object, with different entry directions enabled. If you're placing a &quot;common writing chair&quot; such that there's a solid object just to its right, then use the &lt;code&gt;CommonWritingChair01L&lt;/code&gt; variant, which can only be entered from and exited to the left.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
          </property>
         </widget>
        </item>
       </layout>
      </widget>
     </item>
